    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="18b">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:ICn">I Cn</ref> 18b</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="18b" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="298" ed="#liebermann"/>Ác þonne we sceolan<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="15" target="#ICn_18b.lnote.oe.15"/> habban anfeald lean ðæs þe we on life ær geworhtan<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="13" target="#ICn_18b.lnote.oe.13"/>; wá þam þon<hi rend="italic">ne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="16" target="#ICn_18b.lnote.oe.16"/></hi>, þe ær geearnode<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="17" target="#ICn_18b.lnote.oe.17"/> hellewite!</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="18b" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="298" ed="#liebermann"/>Ac we ðonne sceolon habban anfeald lean ðæs ðe we on life ær geworhton; wa ða<hi rend="italic">m</hi> ðonne, ðe ær geærnode hellewíte!</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="18b" corresp="#wit-a"><pb n="298" ed="#liebermann"/>Ac þonne we sculon anfeald lean habban þæs þe we on life ær geworhton; wa þa<hi rend="italic">m</hi>, þe ær geearnode hellewite!</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="18b" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="299" ed="#liebermann"/>Tunc sane recipiemus<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#ICn_18b.lnote.oe.8"/> uniforme stipendium secundum opera nostra; ue illi<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#ICn_18b.lnote.oe.9"/> qui promeruerit<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#ICn_18b.lnote.oe.10"/> sempiterna<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="11" target="#ICn_18b.lnote.oe.11"/> supplicia!</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="18b" corresp="#wit-cons-cn"><pb n="299" ed="#liebermann"/>Sed nobis debebitur retribucio, secundum quod promeremur, dum uiuimus; ue tunc illi qui ante promeruit supplicium inferni!</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="18b" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="168" ed="#robertson"/>Ac we ðonne sceolon habban anfeald lean ðæs ðe we on life ær geworhton; wa ðam ðonne ðe ær geærnode hellewíte!</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#rabin-doml" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="246" ed="#rabin-doml"/>Ac þonne we sceolan habban anfeald lean ðæs þe we on life ær geworhtan. Wa þam þonne þe ær geearnode hellewite!</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="299" ed="#liebermann"/>Aber dann<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#ICn_18b.lnote.de.1"/> werden wir einfachen Lohn dafür, was wir im Leben zuvor gethan haben, empfangen; wehe<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#ICn_18b.lnote.de.2"/> dann dem, der früher Höllenstrafe verdient hat!</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">But then we shall have a single reward for what we did before in life; woe then to him who has earned hell-torment!</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b"><ptr type="editorNote" n="1" target="#ICn_18b.robnote.n.1"/><pb n="169" ed="#robertson"/>But we shall then receive simply<ptr type="editorNote" n="2" target="#ICn_18b.robnote.n.2"/> the reward for what we have done in our lifetime. Woe then to him who has earned the torment of hell!<ptr type="editorNote" n="3" target="#ICn_18b.robnote.n.3"/></ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#rabin-doml" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="247" ed="#rabin-doml"/>But then we will receive a reward fitting for that which we previously accomplished during our lifetime. Woe then to that person who has earned the pains of hell!</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#liebermann" n="299">Wulfstan p. 114</note>
        <note type="comparisonPrinted" source="#liebermann">ebd.</note>
        <note type="editorial">Robertson (p. 139) takes I Cn from B, which begins at ‘mæssedæg’ in 14.2, the earlier clauses being supplied from G; BR: added from Gesetze I p. 299, October 2026 (MS Cons)</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="ICn_18b.lnote.oe.15" n="15" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">298</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">sculon a. l. h. <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">sculon a. l. h. Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="ICn_18b.lnote.oe.13" n="13" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">298</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-ton <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-ton Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="ICn_18b.lnote.oe.16" n="16" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">298</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">lacking in Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="ICn_18b.lnote.oe.17" n="17" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">298</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">geærn. <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">geærn. Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="ICn_18b.lnote.oe.8" n="8" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">299</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-pimus <hi rend="italic">Dm</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-pimus Dm</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="ICn_18b.lnote.oe.9" n="9" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">299</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">nur K</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">only K</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="ICn_18b.lnote.oe.10" n="10" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">299</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-ruit <hi rend="italic">KT</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-ruit KT</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="ICn_18b.lnote.oe.11" n="11" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">299</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">eterna <hi rend="italic">KT</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">eterna KT</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="ICn_18b.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">200</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Nicht ‘wenn [quando]’ Schmid, denn der zweite Satz steht in Homilien auch allein. Napier <hi rend="italic">Wulfstan</hi> 114 trennt beide Sätze</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Not ‘when [quando]’, as Schmid, for the second sentence also stands on its own in homilies. Napier <hi rend="italic">Wulfstan</hi> 114 separates the two sentences</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="ICn_18b.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">200</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">So hinter vorigem Satz <hi rend="italic">Homil</hi>. 114; alleinstehend 94.—Dieser Gedanke, wie <hi rend="italic">u</hi>. 25. II 84, 1a, häufig in Homilien, auch in Dichtung: <hi rend="italic">on Domdæge Dryhten wile deman, se ah domes geweald, anra gehwylcum, swa he him ærur her on þyssum lænum life geearnað</hi>; ‘Traum vom Kreuz’ 107.—<hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Hölle 2</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Thus, after the preceding sentence, <hi rend="italic">Homil</hi>. 114; standing alone, 94.—This idea, like <hi rend="italic">below</hi> 25. II 84, 1a, is frequent in homilies, and also in poetry: <hi rend="italic">on Domdæge Dryhten wile deman, se ah domes geweald, anra gehwylcum, swa he him ærur her on þyssum lænum life geearnað</hi>; ‘Dream of the Rood’ 107.—<hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Hölle 2</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="ICn_18b.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">350</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. Wulfstan, p. 114; <hi rend="italic">Dream of the Rood</hi>, l. 107 ff.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="ICn_18b.robnote.n.2" n="2" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">350</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">The meaning of <hi rend="italic">anfeald</hi> is presumably not to be taken too literally.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="ICn_18b.robnote.n.3" n="3" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">350</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. Wulfstan, pp. 94, 114.</seg></note>
      </div>
    </body>
